  "basic_html": "<!doctype html>\n<html>\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\n<title> APOD: 2026 September 13 - Comet NEOWISE Rising Over the Adriatic Sea</title>\n<link rel=\"canonical\" href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-september-13-comet-neowise-rising-over-the-adriatic-sea/\">\n<!-- gsfc meta tags -->\n<meta name=\"orgcode\" content=\"661\">\n<meta name=\"rno\" content=\"Dana Bolles\">\n<meta name=\"content-owner\" content=\"Robert Nemiroff\">\n<meta name=\"webmaster\" content=\"Stephen.F.Fantasia.1\">\n<meta name=\"description\" content=\"Explanation: This sight was worth getting out of bed early. Just over four years ago, Comet C/2020 F3 (NEOWISE) rose before dawn to the delight of northern sky enthusiasts awake that early. Up before sunrise on July 8th, the featured photographer was able to capture in dramatic fashion one of the few comets visible to the unaided eye this century, an inner-Solar System intruder that has become known as the Great Comet of 2020. The resulting video detailed Comet NEOWISE from Italy rising over the Adriatic Sea. The featured time-lapse video combines over 240 images taken over 30 minutes. The comet was seen rising through a foreground of bright and undulating noctilucent clouds, and before a background of distant stars. Comet NEOWISE remained unexpectedly bright for over a month, with its ion and dust tails found to emanate from a nucleus spanning about five kilometers across.APOD&#039;s main NASA site is moving: From apod.nasa.gov to science.nasa.gov/apodTomorrow&#039;s picture: open space\">\n<!-- -->\n<meta name=\"keywords\" content=\"comet neowise rising over the adriatic sea\">\n<meta property=\"og:type\" content=\"article\">\n<meta property=\"og:title\" content=\"Comet NEOWISE Rising Over the Adriatic Sea\">\n<meta property=\"og:url\" content=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-september-13-comet-neowise-rising-over-the-adriatic-sea/\">\n<meta property=\"og:site_name\" content=\"NASA Science\">\n<meta property=\"og:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/september/Comet_snapshot.png?w=1080&#038;h=1350&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:secure_url\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/september/Comet_snapshot.png?w=1080&#038;h=1350&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:alt\" content=\"Comet in the sky low to the horizon above the clouds.\">\n<meta property=\"article:published_time\" content=\"2026-09-13T00:05:00-04:00\">\n<meta property=\"article:modified_time\" content=\"2026-09-11T17:18:17-04:00\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:card\" content=\"summary_large_image\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:title\" content=\"Comet NEOWISE Rising Over the Adriatic Sea\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:site\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:creator\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/september/Comet_snapshot.png?w=1080&#038;h=1350&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n</head>\n<body BGCOLOR=\"#F4F4FF\" text=\"#000000\" link=\"#0000FF\" vlink=\"#7F0F9F\"\nalink=\"#FF0000\">\n\n<center>\n<h1> Astronomy Picture of the Day </h1>\n<p>\n\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Discover the cosmos!</a>\nEach day a different image or photograph of our fascinating universe is\nfeatured, along with a brief explanation written by a professional astronomer.\n<p>\n\n2026 September 13<br>\n\n<video controls title=\"\">\n<source src=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/content/dam/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/september/NoctilucentNeowise_Girotti.mp4\" type=\"video/mp4\">\n</video>\n</center>\n\n<center>\n<b> Comet NEOWISE Rising Over the Adriatic Sea </b> <br>\n\n<b>Image Credit &\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Copyright</a>:</b>\n\n<a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/astrogyres/\">Paolo Girotti</a>\n</center>\n\n<p>\n\n<b> Explanation: </b>\n\n<strong>Explanation: </strong>This sight was worth getting out of bed early. Just over four years ago, <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/C/2020_F3_(NEOWISE)\">Comet C/2020 F3 (NEOWISE)</a> rose before dawn to the <a href=\"https://unsplash.com/photos/brown-tabby-cat-7GX5aICb5i4\">delight</a> of northern sky enthusiasts awake that early. Up before sunrise on July 8th, the featured photographer was able to capture in dramatic fashion one of the few comets <a href=\"https://www.facebook.com/media/set/?vanity=APOD.Sky&amp;set=a.2762832093820992\">visible</a> to the unaided eye this century, an inner-<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/\">Solar System</a> intruder that has become known as the <a href=\"https://apod.nasa.gov/apod/ap131013.html\">Great Comet</a> of 2020. The <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/p/CCXz9yUIOQb/\">resulting video</a> detailed Comet NEOWISE from <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Italy\">Italy</a> rising over the <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adriatic_Sea\">Adriatic Sea</a>. The <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/ts0Ek3nLHew\">featured time-lapse video</a> combines over 240 images taken over 30 minutes. <a href=\"https://apod.nasa.gov/apod/ap200707.html\">The comet</a> was seen rising through a foreground of bright and undulating <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Noctilucent_cloud\">noctilucent clouds</a>, and before a background of distant stars. <a href=\"https://earthsky.org/space/how-to-see-comet-c2020-f3-neowise\">Comet NEOWISE</a> remained unexpectedly bright for over a month, with its <a href=\"https://apod.nasa.gov/apod/ap210308.html\">ion and dust</a> tails <a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/feature/jpl/comet-neowise-sizzles-as-it-slides-by-the-sun-providing-a-treat-for-observers\">found to emanate</a> from a <a href=\"https://apod.nasa.gov/apod/ap100104.html\">nucleus</a> spanning about five kilometers across.<br><br><strong>APOD's main NASA site is <a href=\"https://asterisk.apod.com/viewtopic.php?t=45023\">moving</a>:</strong> From <a href=\"https://apod.nasa.gov/\">apod.nasa.gov</a> to <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod\">science.nasa.gov/apod</a><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture: </strong>open space\n<p> <center>\n\n<b> Tomorrow's picture: </b><a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-september-14-where-your-elements-came-from/\">APOD: 2026 September 14 &#8211; Where Your Elements Came From</a>\n\n<p> <hr>\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-september-12-apollo-11-catching-some-sun/\">&lt;</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Archive</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Index</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Search</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/calendar/\">Calendar</a>\n| <a href=\"/feed/apod-basic/\">RSS</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/education-links/\">Education</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">About APOD</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-september-14-where-your-elements-came-from/\">&gt;</a>\n\n<hr><p>\n<b> Authors & editors: </b>\nRobert Nemiroff, Jerry Bonnell, Cecilia Chirenti, Keighley Rockcliffe<br>\n<b>NASA Official: </b> Dana Bolles<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Specific rights apply</a>.<br>\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/about/highlights/HP_Privacy.html\">NASA Web\nPrivacy Policy and Important Notices</a><br>\n<b>A service of:</b>\n<a href=\"http://astrophysics.gsfc.nasa.gov/\">ASD</a> at\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/\">NASA</a> /\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/centers/goddard/\">GSFC</a>\n<br><b>&</b> <a href=\"http://www.mtu.edu/\">Michigan Tech. U.</a><br>\n</center>\n</body>\n</html>",
